},{"week":13,"opponent":"GB","home":true,"pf":24.0,"pa":31.0,"off_snaps":60,"epa_per_play":0.03874,"pass_rate":0.5,"pass_rate_neutral":0.3333,"def_epa_per_play":0.2888},{"week":14,"opponent":"DAL","home":true,"pf":44.0,"pa":30.0,"off_snaps":55,"epa_per_play":0.3332,"pass_rate":0.6364,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6429,"def_epa_per_play":-0.01346},{"week":15,"opponent":"LA","home":false,"pf":34.0,"pa":41.0,"off_snaps":62,"epa_per_play":0.156,"pass_rate":0.6774,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6316,"def_epa_per_play":0.1972},{"week":16,"opponent":"PIT","home":true,"pf":24.0,"pa":29.0,"off_snaps":68,"epa_per_play":0.1013,"pass_rate":0.8382,"pass_rate_neutral":0.7368,"def_epa_per_play":0.2117},{"week":17,"opponent":"MIN","home":false,"pf":10.0,"pa":23.0,"off_snaps":64,"epa_per_play":-0.4637,"pass_rate":0.5312,"pass_rate_neutral":0.463,"def_epa_per_play":-0.2774},{"week":18,"opponent":"CHI","home":false,"pf":19.0,"pa":16.0,"off_snaps":72,"epa_per_play":0.1443,"pass_rate":0.625,"pass_rate_neutral":0.7222,"def_epa_per_play":-0.01936}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"HOU","distance":0.5124,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Bobby Slowik"},{"season":2025,"team":"MIN","distance":0.5594,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kevin O'Connell"},{"season":2023,"team":"LV","distance":0.6192,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"TB","distance":0.6458,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Liam Coen"},{"season":2025,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6918,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Liam Coen"}],"defense":[{"season":2020,"team":"JAX","distance":0.7941,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"DEN","distance":0.9053,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"CLE","distance":1.085,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"JAX","distance":1.099,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"CLE","distance":1.124,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Lions went 9-8 in 2025 and outscored opponents 481 to 413. The offense ranked 7th in EPA per play and the defense 14th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd lowest pistol usage and the highest base defense usage in the league.","offense":"The Lions offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd lowest pistol usage in the league. Jared Goff ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Amon-Ra St. Brown, who drew 31% of the targets.","defense":"The Lions defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was sack rate, where it ranked 6th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 22nd. Aidan Hutchinson was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Al-Quadin Muhammad, who had 11 sacks despite playing only 41% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest base defense usage in the league.","special":"The Lions were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 56% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 79% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 23% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 87% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Jared Goff played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,564 yards. Amon-Ra St. Brown was the clear top target with 31% of the looks and 1,401 receiving yards. Jahmyr Gibbs handled most of the work in the backfield with 58% of the carries. On defense, Aidan Hutchinson led the team with 14.5 sacks and Kerby Joseph picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"John Morton called the offense and Kelvin Sheppard called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2025 Vikings under Kevin O'Connell. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Jaguars and the 2018 Broncos.","trends":"The offense went from 3rd in EPA per play in 2024 to 7th in 2025, a modest decline. The defense moved from 6th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 14th in 2025, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 0 rate, which went from 5% to 2%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}